WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.140
Fat comes apart two carbons at a time, as acetyl-CoA.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.160
Now stretch that over a long fast.

3
00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:13.560
In 1965, Angus Barbieri stopped eating for 382 days.

4
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.320
How did his brain keep running?

5
00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:24.727
Under medical supervision, he lived on water, tea and coffee, with vitamins.

6
00:00:26.167 --> 00:00:29.467
He went from 456 pounds to 180.

7
00:00:30.887 --> 00:00:33.447
Early in a fast, the brain depends on glucose.

8
00:00:34.887 --> 00:00:36.507
So where could glucose come from?

9
00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:40.307
Not from even-chain fat, by the usual route.

10
00:00:41.587 --> 00:00:46.807
Each turn of the citric acid cycle takes in two carbons as acetyl-CoA.

11
00:00:47.987 --> 00:00:50.087
It loses two as carbon dioxide.

12
00:00:51.587 --> 00:00:54.267
No oxaloacetate is left over to build glucose.

13
00:00:55.417 --> 00:01:00.417
Only the glycerol backbone and the tail of odd chains make net sugar from fat.

14
00:01:01.757 --> 00:01:03.917
Most of fat's carbon needs another route.

15
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.440
When glucose is scarce, low insulin floods the liver with fatty acids.

16
00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.300
It opens the carnitine gate.

17
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.900
Beta β-oxidation then makes acetyl-CoA faster than the liver's citric acid cycle can

18
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:20.900
burn it.

19
00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:23.530
The liver repackages the surplus.

20
00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:29.430
Two acetyl-CoA become one acetoacetate, a ketone body.

21
00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:35.250
It can be reduced to D-3-hydroxybutyrate, the dominant form in blood.

22
00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:41.413
Here is the idea. Even-chain fat cannot become net glucose.

23
00:01:42.753 --> 00:01:47.993
So in a fast, the liver ships fat's two-carbon units as ketone bodies, fuel for

24
00:01:47.993 --> 00:01:48.993
other tissues.

25
00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:51.287
Shipping costs the liver.

26
00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:58.767
If it exports palmitate as four acetoacetate, it keeps about 26 of the 106 ATP.

27
00:02:00.327 --> 00:02:03.587
The tissues that burn the ketones collect most of the rest.

28
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:09.130
Those capsules still hold most of the energy, and the liver made every one.

29
00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.230
Will the liver burn its own ketones?

30
00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:25.607
No. The liver lacks thiophorase, the enzyme that reloads acetoacetate onto coenzyme

31
00:02:25.607 --> 00:02:31.307
A. Red blood cells cannot use ketones either, having no mitochondria.

32
00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:36.407
Heart, muscle, kidney and, after adaptation, the brain run on them.

33
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.700
George Cahill's starvation studies measured the payoff.

34
00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:47.400
By day 40, protein breakdown fell from roughly 75 to 20 grams a day.

35
00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.560
The brain had shifted onto ketones, sparing muscle.

36
00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:55.150
That is how a year of fat could feed a brain.

37
00:02:56.410 --> 00:03:01.130
By Cahill's data, Barbieri's brain would have run largely on ketones from his own

38
00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:06.070
fat. Gluconeogenesis would have supplied the glucose it still needed.

39
00:03:07.133 --> 00:03:09.653
The even-chain carbons made no net sugar.

40
00:03:10.893 --> 00:03:15.513
The liver turned them into a fuel it cannot burn, and sent it to tissues that can.
